.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH dfdu 1  "dfdu 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
dfdu \- Disk usage browser for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdfdu\fR [\fB\-x\fR|\fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-cross\-mounts\fR] [\fB\-\-network\fR] [\fB\-\-mft\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-rules\fR] [\fB\-\-disks\fR] [\fB\-\-resume\-from\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-read\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\-agent\fR] [\fB\-\-type\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-history\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-bars\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIPATH\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Disk usage browser for the terminal
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR
Stay on the filesystem of the scanned directory (the default; overrides \-\-cross\-mounts and \-\-network given before it)
.TP
\fB\-\-cross\-mounts\fR
Descend into other local filesystems (disks, tmpfs)
.TP
\fB\-\-network\fR
Descend into network filesystems (NFS, SMB, sshfs and other FUSE network mounts); with or without \-\-cross\-mounts
.TP
\fB\-\-mft\fR
Windows, as administrator: read each NTFS volume\*(Aqs master file table instead of listing its directories, which is much faster on large volumes. Volumes whose table cannot be read are listed as usual
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-rules\fR
Do not classify entries: no categories, no cleanup candidates
.TP
\fB\-\-disks\fR
Choose a disk to browse from the mounted filesystems
.TP
\fB\-\-resume\-from\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Open the scan saved in FILE (a snapshot or an ncdu export) at once, and rescan its directory in the background, replacing it when done. A snapshot is rescanned with the mount options it was taken with
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Browse a scan saved in FILE (a snapshot, an ncdu JSON export, plain or zstd, or an ncdu binary export from `ncdu \-O`; `\-` for stdin), read\-only
.TP
\fB\-\-read\-only\fR
Never delete anything: the browser leaves out its delete keys and runs cleanup commands only as dry runs
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<HOST>\fR
Browse PATH on HOST, scanned there by the dfdu agent through ssh (sent there if HOST has none, see \-\-remote\-agent) and classified with the rules of this host. The delete keys delete on HOST, unless \-\-read\-only; nothing is recorded in the history
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\-agent\fR \fI<HOW>\fR
How to start the agent on HOST: auto (an installed dfdu that speaks this one\*(Aqs protocol, else one sent earlier, else send this version\*(Aqs, asking first), installed, or upload (send one without asking) [default: auto, or DFDU_REMOTE_AGENT]
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: The installed dfdu if it speaks this one\*(Aqs protocol, else one sent earlier, else send one (asking first)
.IP \(bu 2
installed: The dfdu installed on the host, as `ssh HOST dfdu agent`
.IP \(bu 2
upload: One sent earlier, else send one, without asking
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-type\fR \fI<TYPES>\fR
Show only files of these types or extensions, comma\-separated: `video,audio,.iso` (`t` in the browser changes it)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-history\fR
Do not record the scan in the history, nor show what grew since the last one
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR
Scan PATH afresh: do not open its cached scan, nor save this one (`DFDU_CACHE=0` turns the cache off for good). Without it, the browser opens the last complete scan of PATH at once, if one is cached, and brings it up to date in the background; scans that take 3 s or more are cached for 14 days (`dfdu cache list`)
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\fR \fI<THEME>\fR [default: auto]
Colour theme: auto, dark, light, ansi (the terminal\*(Aqs own 16 colours), mono, high\-contrast, or NAME for the theme file NAME.toml in dfdu/themes in the configuration directory. auto asks the terminal for its background colour at start (up to 1 s if it does not answer) and picks dark or light, or ansi when the colour is unknown
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<WHEN>\fR [default: auto]
Colours: auto (from the terminal and NO_COLOR), never, 16, 256 or truecolor. A level given here is used even when NO_COLOR is set
.TP
\fB\-\-bars\fR \fI<STYLE>\fR
Bars: unicode (eighth blocks, the default) or ascii (# and =, for terminals and fonts without block characters). Overrides the theme file\*(Aqs `bars`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Do not capture the mouse (click to select, double\-click to open, wheel to scroll). While it is captured, most terminals select text with Shift held down
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fIPATH\fR]
Directory to browse (opens the interactive browser when stdout is a terminal)
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
dfdu\-scan(1)
Scan a directory and print a summary
.TP
dfdu\-disks(1)
List the mounted filesystems with their size and free space
.TP
dfdu\-report(1)
Scan a directory and list what the cleanup rules found in it: caches, build output, dependencies, with how safe each is to delete
.TP
dfdu\-clean(1)
Delete what `report` recommends (dry run unless \-\-yes)
.TP
dfdu\-diff(1)
What grew: a directory now against its last recorded scan (or one from \-\-since ago), or two saved scans
.TP
dfdu\-history(1)
List the recorded scans
.TP
dfdu\-dupes(1)
Scan a directory and list the files with identical contents (size, then partial hash, then SHA\-256), the most space to free first
.TP
dfdu\-export(1)
Scan a directory and write it in ncdu\*(Aqs JSON format
.TP
dfdu\-import(1)
Read a scan in ncdu\*(Aqs JSON format and print a summary (`dfdu \-f FILE` browses it)
.TP
dfdu\-recover(1)
List what earlier sessions left unfinished (staged items, interrupted deletes), and finish, restore or dismiss them
.TP
dfdu\-cache(1)
List or clear the scans the browser cached, which it opens at once next time
.TP
dfdu\-completions(1)
Print a shell completion script (bash, zsh, fish, elvish, powershell)
.TP
dfdu\-agent(1)
Scan for a dfdu on another host (`\-\-remote`): reads requests on stdin and writes what it finds to stdout in dfdu\*(Aqs wire format. Not for use by hand
.TP
dfdu\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH EXTRA
Exit codes:
  0    success
  1    fatal error (the directory could not be read, an output could not be written)
  2    usage error
  3    finished, but some entries could not be read or the scan is incomplete
  4    report \-\-fail\-above: the matches add up to more than the limit
  5    clean, recover: some deletions failed or were refused
  130  interrupted (Ctrl\-C)
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
